knowledge "testing" {
    purpose "What a check establishes and what it does not: where verification is pointed, how a run is read, what a passing result proves, and how a comparison is set up so its number means something. Reusable across projects and toolchains."
}

ruling "verification-follows-the-change" {
    pack "testing"
    statement "Run the checks that cover what changed: the tests for the touched modules, the type check for the touched package, the lint for the touched files. A whole-suite run to verify a one-module edit is cost without information, and running the full gate after every edit trains it to be a ritual rather than evidence. The full gate belongs at an integration point, where a result about the whole tree is the result being asked for."
}

ruling "read-the-whole-run" {
    pack "testing"
    statement "A run is evidence only when its whole report was read: the exit code first, the failed block second, the summary last. Failures print before the summary in most runners, and a passing count prints even on a red run, so reading only the tail shows a green looking line on a failure. A red run is re-read before it is re-run; the re-run is for the fix and the read is for the truth."
}

ruling "green-proves-only-what-it-checked" {
    pack "testing"
    statement "A passing check is authoritative about what it exercised and silent about everything else. No violation found and the behavior was exercised are different claims, and a suite that never reaches a state cannot report anything about it. Before trusting a green result, ask which obligation it actually reached, and treat a check that could never fail as uninformative rather than satisfied."
}

ruling "assert-structure-not-text" {
    pack "testing"
    statement "Assert identifiers, structural attributes, counts, roles and values the code exposes, never user-facing or translatable wording. A text assertion turns every wording change into a failing suite and teaches the team that a red run is normal. Where a formatted message must be checked, build the expectation through the same function the code uses instead of writing the literal a second time."
}

ruling "verification-is-not-progress" {
    pack "testing"
    statement "Adding checks is not implementing. When work stalls, writing more verification is the most common displacement activity: it feels productive, it is easy to justify, and it leaves the actual obligation untouched. Verification earns its place by the failure it would catch, not by its presence."
}

ruling "one-variable-per-comparison" {
    pack "testing"
    statement "A comparison that changes two things measures neither. Hold the model, the tooling, the task, the environment and the baseline fixed, and vary only the thing under test; anything else produces a number that cannot be attributed. A factor already known to dominate the result is held constant rather than studied again inside the same run, and a question about that factor is a separate experiment."
}
